{
  "about": "Time words and counts in a message, one rule for Express and the agent service (docs/02 §2.11.1 rule 5, docs/04 §4.7). Express reads it in server/test/period.test.js (T41, TC-F14-25); the agent service's pytest reads the same file when period.py is built (T45). Each row gives a message `text`, the phrases `found` in it, in the order they are typed, each with its `kind` and its words exactly as typed, and `rest`, the words that no phrase took, joined by one space. A phrase's numbers are never an amount. Matching rules: the text is split on whitespace after a leading '+'; a word is compared in lower case without accents and without punctuation around it, so 'Ngày?' reads as 'ngay' (the one exception: 'ngày' needs its accent, see 'days'); a number is only digits, and a number followed by a money unit written apart ('k', 'nghìn', 'tr', 'triệu', 'm', 'ng', 'ngàn', 'đ', 'vnd') or joined to it ('10k') is money, never part of a phrase. Kinds: 'day' = hôm nay, hôm qua, hqua, yesterday; 'week' = tuần này, tuần trước, cuối tuần; 'month' = tháng này, tháng trước, tháng N (N 1-12, two digits at most), also with 'năm YYYY' after it (YYYY 1900-2099); 'year' = năm YYYY (1900-2099) and tháng YYYY (2000-2099); 'date' = dd/mm and dd/mm/yyyy (day 1-31, month 1-12; it only has to look like a date, 31/02 is not checked here); 'days' = N ngày (N 1-366) when 'ngày' is written with its accent (in NFC or NFD form) or when qua, trước or nay follows it, the word that follows belongs to the phrase; 'items' = N khoản (N 1-50); 'top' = top N (N 1-999) only when 'top' is the first word. `ranges` (read by the agent service's pytest only, T45) says what the first period phrase of a text means on a given `today` (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh, YYYY-MM-DD): `period` is { from, to, unit } with both days included, or null when the text names no period. A week is Monday to Sunday and the whole calendar unit is meant, also the current one: 'tuần này' on Wed 25/11 is 23/11-29/11. 'day' kinds give unit 'day'; 'week', 'month' and 'year' kinds give their unit; a date (dd/mm) is a day: without a year it is the latest such date that is not after `today` (28/11 on 25/11/2026 is 28/11/2025) and a date that does not exist that year (31/02, 29/02 in 2026) is no period; 'tháng N' without a year is the latest such month not after the current one (tháng 12 on 25/11/2026 is 12/2025); 'cuối tuần' is the latest Saturday-Sunday whose Saturday is not after `today`; 'N ngày qua/trước/nay' and 'N ngày' are the N days that end on `today` (unit 'days': not a calendar unit, a comparison shifts it back by its own length). 'top N' and 'N khoản' are no period. When a text has several period phrases the first one counts.",
